<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW - English for Africa</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa</id><updated>2026-11-14T06:45:56Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-africa" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12756?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Senegal Youth Olympics in Dakar end on high note</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b1fdc9c98e8d841ac9db745611c7fbf9b3636e1f2883efd8f47dfa8a7881fd0c</id><updated>2026-11-14T06:45:56Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T04:25:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/senegal-youth-olympics-in-dakar-end-on-high-note/a-79944820--d8ec73fdda7d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/senegal-youth-olympics-in-dakar-end-on-high-note/a-79944820?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Enthusiastic crowds attended the games in Senegal, with China taking home the most gold medals. It's the first Olympic Games since Russia's war on Ukraine where Russian athletes can compete under their own flag.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>After suffering its worst election result, what's next for the ANC — and for South Africa?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d38768ab794639bf5e9238fcc5932426693515bab3c421f2463b3e002d2e02e6</id><updated>2026-11-14T06:45:05Z</updated><published>2026-11-13T08:33:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/after-suffering-its-worst-election-result-what-s-next-for-the-anc-and-for-south-africa/a-79929902--0f5fc83270ef.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/after-suffering-its-worst-election-result-what-s-next-for-the-anc-and-for-south-africa/a-79929902?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A crushing local election result across the country has left South Africa's ruling ANC searching for answers. Experts say the vote reflects public frustration with service delivery failures — signaling a political shift.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria: Axe-wielding men attack opposition campaign rally in Lagos</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:aa64d48ad34aa65b581b0d0b2b77f26c07600460f485486ce64e12b7cd2d47b4</id><updated>2026-11-14T06:45:05Z</updated><published>2026-11-12T19:01:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nigeria-axe-wielding-men-attack-opposition-campaign-rally-in-lagos/a-79934958--41681a64a2a7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nigeria-axe-wielding-men-attack-opposition-campaign-rally-in-lagos/a-79934958?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A group of men armed with axes, clubs and machetes have reportedly attacked an opposition rally in the run-up to presidential elections. Journalists were also attacked as victims pointed fingers at the ruling APC party.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Wael Mohya could make German football history at 17</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:20672002626114fba9573e25626b1a4cdc0642454d19ccc5aacedec002b53bcf</id><updated>2026-11-14T06:45:05Z</updated><published>2026-11-11T15:00:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/wael-mohya-could-make-german-football-history-at-17/a-79919787--677b2c671ea6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/wael-mohya-could-make-german-football-history-at-17/a-79919787?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">At just 17, Gladbach forward Wael Mohya has earned his first Germany call-up. The dual-national talent has chosen the DFB path for now — and could soon make modern national-team history.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Controversial kinship: From colonial rule to the Holocaust?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8a9f3d5e84ef4570563b7e229b4af6215519f7d2b4af2842418eaf4bc2793da0</id><updated>2026-11-14T06:45:05Z</updated><published>2026-11-10T15:49:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/controversial-kinship-from-colonial-rule-to-the-holocaust/a-79902228--16129c6452e2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/controversial-kinship-from-colonial-rule-to-the-holocaust/a-79902228?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Hitler once called Eastern Europe "Germany's India." A new exhibition examines the links between colonialism and Nazism, and the bitter debate over where comparisons illuminate history and where they fall short.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry></feed>
